INSERT INTO questions (category, sub_category, title, question_text, tables_json, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, status, originality_note, passage_group_id) VALUES ('tgweb_english', '長文問題', '海洋プラスチック回収技術', 'Ocean plastic pollution has become one of the most visible environmental challenges of recent decades, with millions of tons of plastic waste entering the sea each year. Much of this debris breaks down into microplastics, tiny fragments that are difficult to remove and can be consumed by marine animals, entering the food chain. While public awareness of the issue has grown substantially, effective large-scale removal methods have remained limited.

Dr. Hassan Ibrahim, a marine engineer, has spent the past several years developing floating collection systems designed to capture plastic debris in ocean currents before it breaks into smaller pieces. His team’s systems use long floating barriers that funnel debris toward collection points, where it can be removed and recycled. When Ibrahim began this project in 2015, early prototypes could only operate effectively in calm coastal waters. Today, improved versions have been tested successfully in stronger open-ocean currents.

A major turning point came in 2020, when a materials scientist on Ibrahim’s team, Priya Nair, developed a more flexible barrier material that could withstand rough waves without breaking apart, a problem that had damaged several earlier prototypes. This innovation extended the operational lifespan of the collection systems significantly and allowed them to be deployed in more challenging ocean environments, including areas with strong currents where plastic tends to accumulate.

Ibrahim emphasizes that collection systems alone cannot solve ocean plastic pollution; reducing the amount of plastic entering the ocean in the first place remains equally important. Still, he believes that combining prevention efforts with efficient removal technology gives the best chance of addressing the problem. His team is now working with several coastal governments to install permanent collection systems near major river mouths, where much ocean plastic originates, aiming to intercept debris before it reaches open water.

【設問】
What is one problem caused by ocean plastic breaking into microplastics?', NULL, '["It becomes easier to collect and recycle.","It can be consumed by marine animals and enter the food chain.","It sinks immediately to the ocean floor and disappears.","It dissolves completely within a few days."]', 1, '["第1段落「マイクロプラスチックは海洋生物に取り込まれ食物連鎖に入る」。"]', 4, 'published', 'ユーザー提供のオリジナル問題（TG-WEB新型英語: 長文問題・サイエンス時事編）', 912);
