INSERT INTO questions (category, sub_category, title, question_text, tables_json, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, status, originality_note, passage_group_id) VALUES ('tgweb_english', '長文問題', 'サンゴ礁の再生研究', 'Coral reefs, often called the rainforests of the sea due to their biodiversity, have suffered significant damage in recent decades due to rising ocean temperatures, a process that causes coral bleaching. When water becomes too warm, corals expel the colorful algae living within their tissues, turning white and losing a crucial energy source, which can lead to coral death if conditions do not improve quickly enough.

Dr. Aiko Tanaka has spent over a decade researching methods to help damaged reefs recover more quickly than they would naturally. Her team grows coral fragments in controlled nurseries before transplanting them onto damaged reef areas, a technique intended to speed up reef recovery. When Tanaka began this work in 2013, survival rates for transplanted coral fragments were relatively low, often below 40 percent within the first year.

In 2018, a marine biologist on Tanaka’s team, Carlos Mendez, discovered that exposing coral fragments to slightly elevated temperatures in a controlled nursery setting, before transplanting them onto the reef, appeared to increase their tolerance to heat stress later on. This process, sometimes described as a form of stress training, resulted in significantly higher survival rates for transplanted coral compared with fragments that had not undergone the same preparation.

Tanaka’s team is now working to determine which coral species respond best to this heat-tolerance training and how the technique might be applied across larger reef restoration projects. She acknowledges that restoration efforts cannot fully offset the effects of continued ocean warming, but she believes that combining these techniques with broader efforts to reduce climate change could give damaged reefs a better chance of long-term survival. Tanaka hopes to eventually train local communities near vulnerable reefs to carry out simplified versions of this coral nursery technique themselves.

【設問】
What causes coral bleaching, as described in the passage?', NULL, '["Rising ocean temperatures causing corals to expel colorful algae.","Excessive rainfall diluting ocean salt levels.","An increase in the number of fish living near reefs.","A decrease in ocean water temperature."]', 0, '["第1段落「海水温上昇によりサンゴが藻類を排出」。"]', 4, 'published', 'ユーザー提供のオリジナル問題（TG-WEB新型英語: 長文問題・サイエンス時事編）', 915);
