INSERT INTO questions (category, sub_category, title, question_text, tables_json, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, status, originality_note, passage_group_id) VALUES ('tgweb_english', '長文問題', '都市のヒートアイランド対策', 'Urban heat islands, areas within cities that experience significantly higher temperatures than surrounding rural regions, have become a growing concern as cities expand and global temperatures rise. This effect occurs largely because concrete, asphalt, and buildings absorb and retain heat more effectively than natural landscapes, and it can worsen health risks during heat waves, particularly for elderly residents and those without access to air conditioning.

Dr. Amara Osei has spent several years studying how increasing green spaces within cities can help reduce urban heat island effects. Her team measures temperature differences between areas with extensive tree cover or green roofs and nearby areas dominated by concrete and asphalt, aiming to quantify how much cooling benefit different types of green infrastructure actually provide. When Osei began this research in 2018, most city planning decisions regarding green spaces were based on aesthetic or recreational value rather than measured temperature data.

In 2021, an urban planner on Osei’s team, Felix Ndiaye, found that strategically placed street trees providing continuous shade over sidewalks reduced surrounding pavement temperatures more effectively than isolated trees or small park areas spread throughout a neighborhood, even when the total number of trees was similar. This finding suggested that the arrangement of green infrastructure, not just its overall quantity, significantly affects cooling outcomes.

Osei’s team is now working with city planners in several cities to redesign street tree placement based on these findings, prioritizing continuous shade coverage along frequently used pedestrian routes rather than scattering trees without a specific cooling strategy. She believes that combining this research with broader efforts to reduce carbon emissions offers the best approach to protecting city residents from worsening heat risks. Osei hopes her findings can help cities design more effective, budget-conscious green infrastructure plans as urban heat continues to intensify.

【設問】
Why do urban heat islands occur, according to the passage?', NULL, '["Because concrete, asphalt, and buildings absorb and retain heat more than natural landscapes.","Because cities have too many trees and green spaces.","Because rural areas are warmer than cities.","Because air conditioning use lowers overall city temperatures."]', 0, '["第1段落「コンクリートやアスファルトが熱を吸収・保持しやすい」。"]', 4, 'published', 'ユーザー提供のオリジナル問題（TG-WEB新型英語: 長文問題・サイエンス時事編）', 920);
